course: dictionary
lesson: 10187
title: Timber: wood, beams and a warning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Timber means wood that has been cut and is ready to use for building or making things. «Mesopotamia had little wood, stone or metal, so merchants traded grain and cloth for timber, copper and precious stones from far away.» The clue is that timber was valuable enough to trade for, so it must be a building material.
explain: Timber can also mean the large wooden beams of a building or ship, the strong pieces that hold up the frame. Old houses had timber frames, so you can picture the beams as the skeleton of the house. Ask yourself: is the text talking about a material, or a part of a thing? The words around the word show you which.
explain: Look out for the shout «Timber!». It is a short, sudden word on its own. Tree-cutters shout it to warn people that a tree is falling. If the words around it are about trees, workers and danger, the meaning is the warning, not the wood.
explain: Some words change from place to place. Americans say lumber for cut wood, but in the UK and Australia the usual word is timber. When you read something from another country, check the context first, and use a dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: Timber means wood that is cut and used for building or making things.
fact: Timbers are the large wooden beams of a building or ship.
fact: «Timber!» is a warning shout made by tree-cutters when a tree is falling.
fact: In the UK and Australia timber is the usual word; Americans say lumber.
fact: A tree adds one ring of wood each year, and scientists use rings in old timbers to date buildings.
exercise: «Mesopotamia had little wood, stone or metal, so merchants traded grain and cloth for timber, copper and precious stones from far away.» What is timber in this sentence? | A shout of warning | *Wood cut for building | A precious stone | A kind of cloth
exercise: A tree-cutter shouts «Timber!» as a tree starts to fall. What does the shout mean? | *A warning that a tree is falling | A request for more wood | A kind of bird call | A greeting to friends
exercise: «The old ship had timbers that were thick and strong.» What are the timbers? | Small pieces of paper | Songs sung by sailors | *Large wooden beams that form the frame | Metal coins
test: «A tree adds one ring of wood each year, wide rings mean good wet years and thin rings dry or cold ones.» What do wide rings show? | Trees that are very tall | *Good, wet years | Dry or cold years | Years with no rain at all
test: In a design and technology lesson, pupils work with timber. What does timber mean here? | *Wood that is cut and ready to use | Glue for sticking paper | A kind of paint | A type of metal
exam: «Orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra and are critically endangered, as their forest is cut for timber and palm oil.» According to the sentence, why is the forest cut? | *For timber and palm oil | To build new roads only | To grow more grain | To keep orangutans warm
